1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,487
Part Three explains performance engineering for biomedical research.

2
00:00:03,487 --> 00:00:10,024
The most important message is that faster hardware does not rescue a poorly structured workflow.

3
00:00:10,024 --> 00:00:16,997
A computation that should finish in hours can extend to weeks or months when it repeatedly

4
00:00:16,997 --> 00:00:23,099
scans network storage, opens millions of small files, requests the wrong number of threads,

5
00:00:23,099 --> 00:00:26,150
or runs out of memory and restarts.

6
00:00:26,150 --> 00:00:30,944
We will separate CPU, GPU, RAM, disk capacity, and input-output behavior.

7
00:00:30,944 --> 00:00:36,174
We will also explain streaming versus random access, large versus small files,

8
00:00:36,174 --> 00:00:40,968
compression, node-local scratch, and the tools used to find a bottleneck.

9
00:00:40,968 --> 00:00:44,890
The goal is not maximum speed at any cost.

10
00:00:44,890 --> 00:00:48,813
The goal is predictable, fair, and scientifically correct performance.

11
00:00:48,813 --> 00:00:51,372
Performance is not a single number.

12
00:00:51,372 --> 00:00:56,492
An analysis may be limited by CPU instructions, GPU kernels, memory capacity,

13
00:00:56,492 --> 00:01:01,184
memory bandwidth, network transfer, storage throughput, storage latency, or metadata operations.

14
00:01:01,184 --> 00:01:05,450
Improving a resource that is not limiting usually changes little.

15
00:01:05,450 --> 00:01:12,276
For example, adding CPU cores to a job that waits for thousands of small files can

16
00:01:12,276 --> 00:01:14,409
increase contention without shortening runtime.

17
00:01:14,409 --> 00:01:19,955
Start by describing the data path and measuring where elapsed time is spent.

18
00:01:19,955 --> 00:01:22,941
Then change one variable at a time.

19
00:01:22,941 --> 00:01:29,767
This approach is more reliable than guessing from the reputation of a tool or copying a

20
00:01:29,767 --> 00:01:31,900
resource request from another project.

21
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:34,943
Consider a workflow with five thousand samples.

22
00:01:34,943 --> 00:01:40,596
If a step spends one second on useful computation and twenty seconds opening,

23
00:01:40,596 --> 00:01:45,378
seeking, and closing small files on shared storage, the overhead dominates.

24
00:01:45,378 --> 00:01:52,334
Repeat that pattern across many rules and retries, and a nominally short analysis can occupy the

25
00:01:52,334 --> 00:01:53,639
system for weeks.

26
00:01:53,639 --> 00:02:00,160
Another common pattern is decompressing the same source repeatedly, copying entire directories for each rule,

27
00:02:00,160 --> 00:02:04,508
or requesting one core for software configured to use sixteen.

28
00:02:04,508 --> 00:02:09,725
Performance mistakes multiply by the number of samples, files, and workflow stages.

29
00:02:09,725 --> 00:02:15,812
Estimate scale before launch: number of jobs, files per job, bytes read and written,

30
00:02:15,812 --> 00:02:17,986
and expected runtime per unit.

31
00:02:17,986 --> 00:02:21,737
A CPU node contains cores that execute instruction streams.

32
00:02:21,737 --> 00:02:24,238
Some tools expose a threads option.

33
00:02:24,238 --> 00:02:30,906
Slurm must allocate at least that many CPUs per task, and the command must be told

34
00:02:30,906 --> 00:02:32,156
to use them.

35
00:02:32,156 --> 00:02:37,991
Parallel efficiency usually declines as thread count increases because threads coordinate, share memory bandwidth,

36
00:02:37,991 --> 00:02:39,658
and wait for input.

37
00:02:39,658 --> 00:02:46,326
Two independent samples may run faster as two smaller jobs than as one very wide job.

38
00:02:46,326 --> 00:02:51,744
Measure elapsed time and CPU utilization at several thread counts using representative data.

39
00:02:51,744 --> 00:02:58,412
Never start many background processes outside the allocation, and do not assume that a tool called

40
00:02:58,412 --> 00:02:59,662
multithreaded scales linearly.

41
00:02:59,662 --> 00:03:05,423
GPUs contain many execution units optimized for highly parallel numerical work.

42
00:03:05,423 --> 00:03:12,232
They can accelerate deep learning, image analysis, molecular simulation, and selected sequence-analysis tools.

43
00:03:12,232 --> 00:03:19,041
A CPU program does not become GPU-enabled merely because a GPU is allocated.

44
00:03:19,041 --> 00:03:26,373
Request the correct GPU resource, use software built for the available driver and libraries,

45
00:03:26,373 --> 00:03:28,992
and monitor utilization with nvidia-smi.

46
00:03:28,992 --> 00:03:35,801
Low utilization may indicate tiny batches, CPU preprocessing, slow data loading, insufficient parallelism,

47
00:03:35,801 --> 00:03:37,896
or frequent host-to-device transfers.

48
00:03:37,896 --> 00:03:46,276
GPUs are scarce shared resources, so release them when the GPU portion is complete rather than

49
00:03:46,276 --> 00:03:49,418
reserving one during long CPU-only stages.

50
00:03:49,418 --> 00:03:54,958
RAM holds the active working set: data structures, indices, buffers, and program state.

51
00:03:54,958 --> 00:04:00,497
If a job exceeds its Slurm memory allocation, the job may be terminated.

52
00:04:00,497 --> 00:04:07,314
If the operating system begins swapping, performance can collapse because disk is far slower than RAM.

53
00:04:07,314 --> 00:04:13,280
Requesting far more memory than needed can delay scheduling and reduce capacity for everyone.

54
00:04:13,280 --> 00:04:17,967
Use Slurm accounting to inspect maximum resident memory after representative jobs.

55
00:04:17,967 --> 00:04:23,506
Some tools scale memory with threads or input size, so record the relationship.

56
00:04:23,506 --> 00:04:29,898
A memory leak shows steadily rising use; a normal workload often rises to a plateau.

57
00:04:29,898 --> 00:04:31,837
Disk space is capacity.

58
00:04:31,837 --> 00:04:34,745
Input-output performance describes how data moves.

59
00:04:34,745 --> 00:04:41,046
Four useful dimensions are throughput, latency, input-output operations per second, and metadata rate.

60
00:04:41,046 --> 00:04:46,863
Throughput matters for large sequential reads such as scanning a compressed FASTQ.

61
00:04:46,863 --> 00:04:51,226
Latency matters when a program waits for individual operations.

62
00:04:51,226 --> 00:04:55,104
IOPS matters for many small reads and writes.

63
00:04:55,104 --> 00:05:02,859
Metadata rate matters when listing directories, checking file existence, opening files, or creating thousands of paths.

64
00:05:02,859 --> 00:05:10,615
Network storage is designed for shared durable data, but it cannot provide local-disk behavior to every

65
00:05:10,615 --> 00:05:11,584
job simultaneously.

66
00:05:11,584 --> 00:05:15,462
Match the access pattern to the storage tier.

67
00:05:15,462 --> 00:05:19,263
Streaming input-output reads or writes long contiguous sequences.

68
00:05:19,263 --> 00:05:24,964
This lets storage, operating-system caches, compression libraries, and network protocols work efficiently.

69
00:05:24,964 --> 00:05:32,565
Random input-output jumps among many positions or files and repeatedly pays seek, latency, and metadata costs.

70
00:05:32,565 --> 00:05:39,216
Indexed BAM and VCF access can be appropriate when querying a small genomic interval,

71
00:05:39,216 --> 00:05:46,818
but repeatedly issuing tiny queries across the entire genome can be slower than one sequential pass.

72
00:05:46,818 --> 00:05:53,469
Against network storage, avoid database-like random write patterns and large collections of temporary shards.

73
00:05:53,469 --> 00:06:00,595
Stage active files to node-local disk, process them there, and return only the required outputs.

74
00:06:00,595 --> 00:06:06,464
A directory with hundreds of thousands of files can make listing, tab completion,

75
00:06:06,464 --> 00:06:09,625
backups, workflow discovery, and deletion painfully slow.

76
00:06:09,625 --> 00:06:14,140
Small files also waste storage blocks and require metadata operations.

77
00:06:14,140 --> 00:06:19,557
Many bioinformatics tools create temporary fragments, per-read outputs, or tiny environment files.

78
00:06:19,557 --> 00:06:24,975
Prefer a reasonable directory hierarchy, one result bundle per sample or batch,

79
00:06:24,975 --> 00:06:27,684
and archive cold collections when appropriate.

80
00:06:27,684 --> 00:06:34,456
Do not use tar archives as a substitute for formats that need random indexed access,

81
00:06:34,456 --> 00:06:41,229
but do use structured containers or archives for collections that are transferred and consumed together.

82
00:06:41,229 --> 00:06:46,195
Clean temporary files through workflow rules instead of leaving them indefinitely.

83
00:06:46,195 --> 00:06:48,406
Biomedical data is often compressible.

84
00:06:48,406 --> 00:06:51,502
FASTQ is commonly stored as gzip-compressed FASTQ.

85
00:06:51,502 --> 00:06:55,040
VCF can use block gzip with an index.

86
00:06:55,040 --> 00:06:58,578
BAM and CRAM are compressed binary alignment formats.

87
00:06:58,578 --> 00:07:05,655
Keeping files compressed reduces bytes transferred and stored, which often saves more time than decompression costs.

88
00:07:05,655 --> 00:07:12,289
Avoid repeatedly expanding a file to network storage merely because one command expects a path.

89
00:07:12,289 --> 00:07:19,365
Many tools read compressed input directly, and Unix pipes can stream decompressed data between compatible tools.

90
00:07:19,365 --> 00:07:25,557
Confirm that the chosen compression is splittable or indexable when parallel access is required.

91
00:07:25,557 --> 00:07:31,306
Keep the original compressed source and generate only formats needed for downstream analysis.

92
00:07:31,306 --> 00:07:35,394
Node-local scratch is temporary storage attached to the compute node.

93
00:07:35,394 --> 00:07:41,527
It provides lower latency and avoids making shared storage handle every temporary read and write.

94
00:07:41,527 --> 00:07:46,432
Snakemake can expose the job temporary directory through the standard tmpdir resource.

95
00:07:46,432 --> 00:07:52,973
Shadow rules can execute in an isolated directory, with modes that link or copy the required

96
00:07:52,973 --> 00:07:53,382
inputs.

97
00:07:53,382 --> 00:07:59,514
For tools that perform random access or create many temporary files, use an RCC-tested pattern:

98
00:07:59,514 --> 00:08:05,238
copy the required inputs to local scratch, run the command there, validate the output,

99
00:08:05,238 --> 00:08:07,690
and copy only final results back.

100
00:08:07,690 --> 00:08:13,005
Scratch is temporary, so the workflow must not treat it as durable storage.

101
00:08:13,005 --> 00:08:14,852
Use Slurm accounting first.

102
00:08:14,852 --> 00:08:22,241
S acct shows job state, elapsed time, allocated CPUs, and fields such as maximum resident memory.

103
00:08:22,241 --> 00:08:25,936
S stat reports live statistics for running jobs.

104
00:08:25,936 --> 00:08:32,863
Inside an allocation, time measures elapsed and CPU time, top or h top shows processes,

105
00:08:32,863 --> 00:08:38,866
and tools such as pidstat, vmstat, iostat, or dstat can reveal CPU wait,

106
00:08:38,866 --> 00:08:42,099
memory pressure, and storage activity when available.

107
00:08:42,099 --> 00:08:48,564
N v i d i a s m i reports GPU utilization and memory.

108
00:08:48,564 --> 00:08:52,259
Snakemake benchmark files connect measurements to specific rules.

109
00:08:52,259 --> 00:08:59,648
Interpret metrics together: low CPU plus high I/O wait suggests storage; full memory and rising swap

110
00:08:59,648 --> 00:09:05,651
suggests RAM; low GPU utilization with busy CPUs suggests preprocessing or data loading.

111
00:09:05,651 --> 00:09:08,262
A symptom is not a diagnosis.

112
00:09:08,262 --> 00:09:15,222
Low CPU efficiency may be caused by input-output wait, serial code, thread oversubscription, or frequent synchronization.

113
00:09:15,222 --> 00:09:22,183
A job killed for memory needs either a larger allocation or a method that reduces the

114
00:09:22,183 --> 00:09:23,053
working set.

115
00:09:23,053 --> 00:09:29,579
A GPU at ten percent utilization may be waiting on CPU preprocessing or tiny batches.

116
00:09:29,579 --> 00:09:34,799
A workflow that pauses while discovering files may have a metadata problem.

117
00:09:34,799 --> 00:09:41,325
Build a small representative benchmark, change one factor, and compare elapsed time and resource efficiency.

118
00:09:41,325 --> 00:09:48,285
Stop when performance is adequate and fair; optimization also has engineering cost and can introduce scientific

119
00:09:48,285 --> 00:09:48,720
errors.

120
00:09:48,720 --> 00:09:51,129
The operating rules are straightforward.

121
00:09:51,129 --> 00:09:52,574
Measure before scaling.

122
00:09:52,574 --> 00:09:55,945
Match Slurm requests to the software configuration.

123
00:09:55,945 --> 00:09:58,835
Keep source data compressed where possible.

124
00:09:58,835 --> 00:10:00,762
Prefer sequential streaming access.

125
00:10:00,762 --> 00:10:04,133
Avoid random temporary input-output on network storage.

126
00:10:04,133 --> 00:10:09,432
Stage active files to node-local scratch through a tested Snakemake pattern.

127
00:10:09,432 --> 00:10:11,840
Reduce small-file and directory counts.

128
00:10:11,840 --> 00:10:15,693
Preserve only necessary outputs and clean temporary files.

129
00:10:15,693 --> 00:10:21,955
Use Slurm accounting, Snakemake benchmarks, and operating-system tools to identify the actual bottleneck.

130
00:10:21,955 --> 00:10:25,808
Above all, perform a pilot at realistic scale.

131
00:10:25,808 --> 00:10:33,514
An inefficient workflow multiplied across thousands of samples can turn hours into months and degrade the

132
00:10:33,514 --> 00:10:35,441
shared service for colleagues.

